1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,587
You push the course's own code,
untouched, to a repository of your own.

2
00:00:04,907 --> 00:00:08,149
Soon after, one log says:
unit tests, success.

3
00:00:08,469 --> 00:00:10,389
Integration tests, success.

4
00:00:10,709 --> 00:00:12,352
System tests, success.

5
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:13,973
All tests passed.

6
00:00:15,017 --> 00:00:18,397
And the same file,
run end to end, ends like this:

7
00:00:18,397 --> 00:00:20,628
job Lint and Format Check failed.

8
00:00:20,948 --> 00:00:24,297
Five machines green, one red,
and the whole run red.

9
00:00:24,617 --> 00:00:28,372
The pipeline says every test passed,
and the pipeline failed.

10
00:00:28,692 --> 00:00:29,822
Both are true.

11
00:00:30,067 --> 00:00:33,374
So which machine said no,
and why didn't the summary hear it?

12
00:00:33,694 --> 00:00:36,958
By the end of part 1 you will see this
on your own push.

13
00:00:37,278 --> 00:00:39,453
In part 2 we open every log.

14
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,561
In lecture 9 these checks ran
on your laptop, before a commit.

15
00:00:43,881 --> 00:00:48,081
Today they move to GitHub,
and they run on every push to main,

16
00:00:48,081 --> 00:00:50,452
on fresh machines that nobody owns.

17
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,025
Lecture 21 has four sections:
remote Git hooks,

18
00:00:54,025 --> 00:00:58,764
GitHub webhooks, continuous integration,
and continuous deployment.

19
00:00:59,084 --> 00:01:03,500
We follow it slide by slide,
and the README Part 5 step by step.

20
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:08,181
To follow along, you need
a fresh clone of the course's repository,

21
00:01:08,181 --> 00:01:12,347
Docker running, a GitHub account,
and a repository of your own.

22
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:16,977
No cloud and no keys,
and on a public repository, no bill.

23
00:01:17,217 --> 00:01:22,039
The tracker names the README step,
code panels name their file and lines,

24
00:01:22,039 --> 00:01:25,473
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

25
00:01:25,793 --> 00:01:29,324
The runs you will see ran
right here on fresh machines;

26
00:01:29,324 --> 00:01:32,342
the GitHub pages are drawings,
and they say so.

27
00:01:33,483 --> 00:01:36,619
The lecture opens with a recap:
local Git hooks.

28
00:01:36,939 --> 00:01:41,163
A pre-commit hook runs on your laptop,
before the commit even exists.

29
00:01:41,483 --> 00:01:44,256
A pre-push hook runs
before your commits leave.

30
00:01:44,576 --> 00:01:47,435
In lecture 9 you installed this repo's set.

31
00:01:47,683 --> 00:01:51,181
Here is that chain, with this repo's boxes:

32
00:01:51,181 --> 00:01:52,915
seven hygiene checks,

33
00:01:52,915 --> 00:01:56,631
then Black, then
Flake8, both at 120 columns,

34
00:01:56,631 --> 00:01:58,990
and only on files under src/.

35
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:01,336
The lecture's chain ends with tests.

36
00:02:01,656 --> 00:02:05,198
This repo's hooks run none:
it leaves the tests to CI.

37
00:02:05,433 --> 00:02:06,653
The lecture's catch:

38
00:02:06,653 --> 00:02:10,617
hooks run only on a machine
where someone ran pre-commit install.

39
00:02:10,937 --> 00:02:14,777
A teammate's laptop without them
sends its commit through unchecked.

40
00:02:15,097 --> 00:02:17,102
So the checks depend on the laptop.

41
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:19,182
That's where remote hooks come in:

42
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:21,659
they run on the server
that receives the push.

43
00:02:21,979 --> 00:02:25,231
A pre-receive hook runs
before the push is accepted,

44
00:02:25,231 --> 00:02:26,481
so it can refuse it.

45
00:02:26,801 --> 00:02:29,979
The update hook does the same,
one branch at a time.

46
00:02:30,216 --> 00:02:33,344
A post-receive hook runs
after the code is in,

47
00:02:33,344 --> 00:02:36,744
for integration tests,
notifications and clean-up.

48
00:02:37,064 --> 00:02:39,774
It can report, but it can no longer say no.

49
00:02:40,016 --> 00:02:44,688
One honest note: GitHub.com
doesn't run your own pre-receive scripts.

50
00:02:45,008 --> 00:02:47,082
It gives you two pieces instead:

51
00:02:47,082 --> 00:02:52,006
Actions, which runs after the push,
and branch protection, which can say no.

52
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:54,341
And hooks act only inside Git.

53
00:02:54,661 --> 00:02:58,223
To tell a chat what happened,
the lecture reaches for webhooks.

54
00:02:58,466 --> 00:03:01,453
Now the lecture's motivation,
in three slides.

55
00:03:01,773 --> 00:03:04,695
On your laptop,
the checks fire on every commit.

56
00:03:05,015 --> 00:03:09,432
Then you push, and on the shared repository,
nothing runs them at all.

57
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:11,674
So the checks move to the server:

58
00:03:11,674 --> 00:03:15,021
build a container, check the code,
summarize the tests.

59
00:03:15,341 --> 00:03:19,501
Same idea, new home,
for every push to main from every laptop.

60
00:03:19,821 --> 00:03:22,146
That move is continuous integration.

61
00:03:24,317 --> 00:03:28,598
The lecture defines continuous integration
as a series of scripts

62
00:03:28,598 --> 00:03:31,826
that run automatically
whenever the code changes.

63
00:03:32,146 --> 00:03:34,699
They integrate changes continuously,

64
00:03:34,699 --> 00:03:37,890
test automatically,
enforce coding standards,

65
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:39,592
and run static analysis.

66
00:03:39,834 --> 00:03:41,239
In ordinary software,

67
00:03:41,239 --> 00:03:45,253
that buys faster releases, better code,
and easier teamwork.

68
00:03:45,573 --> 00:03:49,156
Machine learning adds a twist;
we come back to it at the end.

69
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,725
Slide 21 draws it as a loop.

70
00:03:52,045 --> 00:03:56,756
Developers commit to the codebase;
the codebase is built, then tested;

71
00:03:56,756 --> 00:03:59,853
and notifications flow back
to the developers.

72
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:02,670
For now, the way back is a question mark.

73
00:04:02,917 --> 00:04:05,285
The course's pipeline has five stages.

74
00:04:05,605 --> 00:04:10,636
A commit triggers it, the container
is built, Black and Flake8 check the code,

75
00:04:10,636 --> 00:04:13,861
the tests run,
and a summary reports pass or fail.

76
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:18,111
Now the README, Part 5:
GitHub Actions, remote CI.

77
00:04:18,431 --> 00:04:24,153
It says all CI tests run inside
Docker containers using Python 3.11,

78
00:04:24,153 --> 00:04:26,089
the same as production.

79
00:04:26,409 --> 00:04:31,380
This repo has no production yet:
take it as the same image you would deploy.

80
00:04:31,617 --> 00:04:35,777
This one isn't in the README, it's extra:
list the workflows folder.

81
00:04:36,097 --> 00:04:41,942
One file, ci.yml, in .github/workflows,
at the root of the repository.

82
00:04:42,262 --> 00:04:46,316
GitHub looks only there;
a workflow in a subfolder never runs.

83
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:47,550
Open it.

84
00:04:47,787 --> 00:04:51,009
The file gives the workflow a name,
CI Pipeline.

85
00:04:51,329 --> 00:04:54,209
Next comes the key called on,
just the word on.

86
00:04:54,529 --> 00:04:56,875
It lists the events that start the workflow.

87
00:04:57,195 --> 00:05:03,062
Under jobs, each job gets its own machine,
called a runner: runs-on: ubuntu-latest.

88
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,732
Inside a job, steps run in order.

89
00:05:06,052 --> 00:05:11,441
A step is either a shell script, under run,
or a reusable action, under uses,

90
00:05:11,441 --> 00:05:13,004
like actions/checkout,

91
00:05:13,004 --> 00:05:16,271
which copies your repository
onto the machine.

92
00:05:16,591 --> 00:05:20,154
Event, workflow, jobs, steps:
that is the whole grammar.

93
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:23,092
When does the pipeline wake up?

94
00:05:23,412 --> 00:05:26,377
The slide says
on every push or pull request.

95
00:05:26,697 --> 00:05:30,294
The file is more precise:
pushes to main or develop,

96
00:05:30,294 --> 00:05:32,991
and pull requests into main or develop.

97
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:35,452
Push to main, and a runner wakes up.

98
00:05:35,772 --> 00:05:37,372
Push to develop, the same.

99
00:05:37,692 --> 00:05:40,363
Push to a feature branch,
and nothing happens:

100
00:05:40,363 --> 00:05:43,964
on any other branch,
the pipeline waits for your pull request.

101
00:05:44,284 --> 00:05:48,380
A small note: the comment
at the top of the file says main only;

102
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:50,556
the triggers say main and develop.

103
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,800
Quick test.

104
00:05:52,101 --> 00:05:54,427
You push three commits to feature/login.

105
00:05:54,747 --> 00:05:56,069
How many runs start?

106
00:05:56,389 --> 00:05:58,715
Then you open a pull request into main.

107
00:05:59,035 --> 00:06:00,037
How many now?

108
00:06:08,883 --> 00:06:11,656
None for the three pushes:
the branch filter.

109
00:06:11,976 --> 00:06:13,873
One when the pull request opens,

110
00:06:13,873 --> 00:06:17,075
and one more for every later push
while it stays open.

111
00:06:17,317 --> 00:06:19,173
And it doesn't test your branch tip.

112
00:06:19,493 --> 00:06:23,135
GitHub makes a temporary merge
of your branch into main,

113
00:06:23,135 --> 00:06:26,256
and the commit ID the jobs see names
that merge.

114
00:06:26,576 --> 00:06:31,567
What will land is what gets tested:
each merge is tested, as the lecture says.

115
00:06:31,817 --> 00:06:35,145
The lecture warns
that integrating often costs compute.

116
00:06:35,465 --> 00:06:38,056
This file answers with a branch filter,

117
00:06:38,056 --> 00:06:41,844
one build shared by every job,
and an image kept one day.

118
00:06:42,164 --> 00:06:46,160
On private repositories every run
counts against your plan;

119
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:47,582
public ones run free.

120
00:06:47,817 --> 00:06:49,073
And timing matters:

121
00:06:49,073 --> 00:06:52,446
the run starts after GitHub
has accepted your push.

122
00:06:52,766 --> 00:06:56,435
It's the bell, not the gate:
a red run doesn't undo the push.

123
00:06:57,583 --> 00:06:58,853
Next, the README:

124
00:06:58,853 --> 00:07:02,810
6 jobs that run in parallel,
except for dependencies.

125
00:07:03,130 --> 00:07:04,969
Each has an ID and a name:

126
00:07:04,969 --> 00:07:11,122
Build Docker Image, Lint and Format Check,
Unit Tests, Integration Tests, System Tests,

127
00:07:11,122 --> 00:07:12,324
and Test Summary.

128
00:07:12,566 --> 00:07:15,054
Remember the names: GitHub shows them,

129
00:07:15,054 --> 00:07:18,262
not the IDs,
and we'll need them to protect main.

130
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:20,548
Start from the lecture's row of five.

131
00:07:20,868 --> 00:07:24,068
Commit drops out first:
it's the event, not a job.

132
00:07:24,388 --> 00:07:27,758
Build Container stays on the left:
it's the build job.

133
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,389
Now each needs line draws one arrow.

134
00:07:30,709 --> 00:07:32,608
Lint and format needs build.

135
00:07:32,928 --> 00:07:37,451
Unit tests, integration tests
and system tests each need build too.

136
00:07:37,771 --> 00:07:41,033
So after the build,
four jobs run side by side,

137
00:07:41,033 --> 00:07:42,698
each on its own machine.

138
00:07:42,933 --> 00:07:45,557
Test summary needs the three test jobs.

139
00:07:45,877 --> 00:07:48,842
Look closely:
the lint job isn't on that list.

140
00:07:49,162 --> 00:07:51,727
The lecture lists the stages in order;

141
00:07:51,727 --> 00:07:57,397
on GitHub, four of them run side by side,
and the summary listens only to the tests.

142
00:07:57,717 --> 00:07:59,210
Keep that gap in mind.

143
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:02,924
An extra check, not in the README:
a tool called act,

144
00:08:02,924 --> 00:08:07,578
which you don't need, takes the same file
and lists the jobs in stages.

145
00:08:07,898 --> 00:08:09,349
Stage 0, build.

146
00:08:09,669 --> 00:08:11,503
Stage 1, the four checks.

147
00:08:11,823 --> 00:08:13,338
Stage 2, the summary.

148
00:08:13,583 --> 00:08:14,991
Then the key concepts.

149
00:08:15,311 --> 00:08:19,574
Artifact sharing and unique image tags come
with the build job;

150
00:08:19,574 --> 00:08:21,807
parallel execution, you just saw;

151
00:08:21,807 --> 00:08:26,340
conditional execution and network modes come
with the system tests.

152
00:08:26,583 --> 00:08:29,239
The README's screenshot gives the times:

153
00:08:29,239 --> 00:08:33,090
about 2 minutes on the clock,
with four jobs side by side.

154
00:08:33,410 --> 00:08:38,147
On a private repository, each job is billed
and rounded up separately:

155
00:08:38,147 --> 00:08:39,703
about 6 billed minutes.

156
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:41,938
Time to push.

157
00:08:42,258 --> 00:08:45,432
Start from a fresh clone,
not your lecture 9 folder:

158
00:08:45,432 --> 00:08:50,194
that one has the Black fix committed,
and your first run would come out green.

159
00:08:50,514 --> 00:08:54,695
Clone, change into the folder,
and git status says the tree is clean.

160
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,435
Your prompt shows your own user.

161
00:09:00,755 --> 00:09:02,243
Two extra checks first:

162
00:09:02,243 --> 00:09:06,838
the pinned commit is still the head of main
on the course's repository,

163
00:09:06,838 --> 00:09:10,333
and the last two commits
are both called demo class 1.

164
00:09:10,653 --> 00:09:14,216
The second undid a bug planted in class;
we'll run it later.

165
00:09:14,450 --> 00:09:16,775
Now the README block, Push to GitHub.

166
00:09:17,095 --> 00:09:18,909
git add . — no output.

167
00:09:19,229 --> 00:09:22,365
Then git commit -m "Add CI pipeline".

168
00:09:22,685 --> 00:09:26,141
And Git answers:
nothing to commit, working tree clean.

169
00:09:26,383 --> 00:09:29,863
A small slip, and a harmless one:
in a clone or a fork,

170
00:09:29,863 --> 00:09:33,786
the pipeline is already committed,
so there is nothing to add.

171
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:38,342
Before the README's git push,
an extra check: git remote -v.

172
00:09:38,662 --> 00:09:42,844
Origin is still the course's repository,
and you can't write to it.

173
00:09:43,164 --> 00:09:46,449
GitHub would refuse a push from here:
permission denied.

174
00:09:46,683 --> 00:09:49,584
So point origin at a repository you own.

175
00:09:49,904 --> 00:09:55,378
Either fork the course's repository
and enable workflows on its Actions tab,

176
00:09:55,378 --> 00:09:57,755
or create a new empty repository.

177
00:09:58,075 --> 00:10:02,576
Either way, run git remote set-url origin,
with your own address.

178
00:10:02,896 --> 00:10:04,475
Pause and set yours up.

179
00:10:07,683 --> 00:10:10,534
This recording machine can't push to GitHub,

180
00:10:10,534 --> 00:10:14,616
so an empty repository
on the same machine stands in for yours.

181
00:10:14,936 --> 00:10:17,710
From now on we just say your repository.

182
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:19,550
Now the README's git push.

183
00:10:19,870 --> 00:10:21,534
New branch, main -> main.

184
00:10:21,854 --> 00:10:24,734
This push is the event,
and run 1 starts now.

185
00:10:25,054 --> 00:10:26,483
Pause and push yours.

186
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:30,033
On a fork, that commit is already on GitHub,

187
00:10:30,033 --> 00:10:33,636
so git push says Everything up-to-date
and starts nothing.

188
00:10:33,956 --> 00:10:37,138
To get run 1 anyway, push an empty commit:

189
00:10:37,138 --> 00:10:41,380
git commit --allow-empty -m "Trigger CI",
then git push.

190
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:45,519
Or wait, and your first run
then comes with the fix in part 2.

191
00:10:46,867 --> 00:10:51,710
Watch GitHub Actions: go to your repository,
and click the Actions tab.

192
00:10:52,030 --> 00:10:56,766
Your run is listed under the commit message,
with a yellow dot: in progress.

193
00:10:57,086 --> 00:11:00,819
A green checkmark means success;
a red X means failure.

194
00:11:01,139 --> 00:11:02,696
Pause and open yours.

195
00:11:02,934 --> 00:11:07,287
Click it, and GitHub draws the run
as the graph you just built:

196
00:11:07,287 --> 00:11:11,019
build first, four jobs side by side,
the summary last.

197
00:11:11,267 --> 00:11:12,867
A word on how we show it.

198
00:11:13,187 --> 00:11:15,885
We ran every job of this file right here,

199
00:11:15,885 --> 00:11:20,163
each on a brand-new machine
with an empty Docker, as GitHub does.

200
00:11:20,483 --> 00:11:25,006
And a tool called act ran the whole file
a second way, with the same verdict.

201
00:11:25,250 --> 00:11:29,497
So the GitHub pages you see
are drawings built from the workflow file,

202
00:11:29,497 --> 00:11:30,711
marked illustration.

203
00:11:31,031 --> 00:11:32,973
Every log line in them is real.

204
00:11:33,217 --> 00:11:35,457
A question, before the colors come in.

205
00:11:35,777 --> 00:11:37,782
You already know this run turns red.

206
00:11:38,102 --> 00:11:42,881
Is your push on main in your repository now,
or did the red run refuse it?

207
00:11:51,717 --> 00:11:52,998
Yes, it's on main:

208
00:11:52,998 --> 00:11:57,840
the push landed before any job started,
and a red run can't undo it.

209
00:11:58,160 --> 00:11:59,461
Build: green.

210
00:11:59,781 --> 00:12:01,872
Lint and Format Check: red.

211
00:12:02,192 --> 00:12:04,944
The three tests and Test Summary: green.

212
00:12:05,264 --> 00:12:06,586
The run: red.

213
00:12:06,834 --> 00:12:11,293
Those checks and crosses next to your commit
are the lecture's notifications.

214
00:12:11,613 --> 00:12:16,284
And act, the second witness, agrees:
five jobs succeeded, one failed.

215
00:12:16,534 --> 00:12:19,734
A kind note:
the README's screenshot is all green.

216
00:12:20,054 --> 00:12:22,337
It shows an earlier version of the code.

217
00:12:22,657 --> 00:12:25,984
Your first run is red,
and in part 2 you turn it green.

218
00:12:26,234 --> 00:12:31,034
And here is the hook, on your own push:
two of these six machines disagree.

219
00:12:31,354 --> 00:12:34,852
In part 2 we open every log,
one job at a time.

220
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:38,099
Part 2, job by job.

221
00:12:38,419 --> 00:12:41,875
First, build container:
the lecture builds it first,

222
00:12:41,875 --> 00:12:47,059
so every check runs in the same environment,
and build problems show up early.

223
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:49,924
The lecture speaks of one remote CI server.

224
00:12:50,244 --> 00:12:53,226
GitHub gives you a new one for every job:

225
00:12:53,226 --> 00:12:56,644
runs-on:
ubuntu-latest, six times in this file.

226
00:12:56,964 --> 00:12:59,183
Each job starts on an empty machine.

227
00:12:59,467 --> 00:13:02,428
The README's build job
builds the image once,

228
00:13:02,428 --> 00:13:04,139
tagged with the commit ID.

229
00:13:04,459 --> 00:13:08,350
It saves it as a tar file
and uploads it as an artifact,

230
00:13:08,350 --> 00:13:11,477
so every other job gets
the exact same image.

231
00:13:11,717 --> 00:13:15,152
On GitHub, click a job,
then a step, to open its log.

232
00:13:15,472 --> 00:13:22,128
Docker builds the image from the Dockerfile,
and names it cheese-app-api:<commit ID>.

233
00:13:22,448 --> 00:13:25,370
That tag means this commit's image,
nothing else.

234
00:13:25,617 --> 00:13:28,997
A small slip:
the README promises Buildx caching,

235
00:13:28,997 --> 00:13:33,756
but the build runs on the default builder,
and nothing saves a cache,

236
00:13:33,756 --> 00:13:36,860
so every fresh machine
rebuilds from scratch.

237
00:13:37,180 --> 00:13:38,822
The exercises show how.

238
00:13:39,067 --> 00:13:44,571
docker save folds the image into one file,
about 176 megabytes.

239
00:13:44,891 --> 00:13:48,838
The upload step carries it
to the artifact store, kept for one day.

240
00:13:49,158 --> 00:13:51,077
Then the build machine is thrown away.

241
00:13:51,317 --> 00:13:53,322
Now step into a fresh check job.

242
00:13:53,642 --> 00:13:57,120
An extra command,
docker images, shows just the header.

243
00:13:57,440 --> 00:13:58,698
No image at all.

244
00:13:58,933 --> 00:14:04,654
So every check job downloads the artifact
and runs docker load -i on the tar:

245
00:14:04,654 --> 00:14:07,701
the same tag, unpacked from the suitcase.

246
00:14:08,021 --> 00:14:09,557
That's artifact sharing.

247
00:14:09,877 --> 00:14:12,278
And the artifact expires after a day,

248
00:14:12,278 --> 00:14:16,043
so re-running a failed job
tomorrow fails at the download.

249
00:14:16,283 --> 00:14:17,648
Your turn to predict.

250
00:14:17,968 --> 00:14:21,680
Delete the "Load Docker image"
step from the unit tests job.

251
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,241
Which step fails, and with what error?

252
00:14:33,083 --> 00:14:34,619
The "Run unit tests" step.

253
00:14:34,939 --> 00:14:38,908
Docker looks for the image
on the machine and finds nothing,

254
00:14:38,908 --> 00:14:42,197
then asks Docker Hub,
where no such image exists:

255
00:14:42,197 --> 00:14:43,472
pull access denied.

256
00:14:43,792 --> 00:14:48,401
Exit code 125:
the step fails, so the job fails.

257
00:14:49,950 --> 00:14:51,507
Next, lint and format.

258
00:14:51,827 --> 00:14:54,736
The lecture's coding standards
are Black here,

259
00:14:54,736 --> 00:14:57,203
and its static code analysis is Flake8.

260
00:14:57,523 --> 00:15:01,189
Remember lecture 9:
a formatter rewrites your code;

261
00:15:01,189 --> 00:15:02,771
a linter only reports.

262
00:15:03,017 --> 00:15:08,329
Both run inside the image,
on the api/ folder, at 120 columns.

263
00:15:08,649 --> 00:15:12,190
The Black step says continue-on-error,
false: it blocks.

264
00:15:12,510 --> 00:15:17,907
The Flake8 step says true: it's advisory,
a warning that never fails the job.

265
00:15:18,150 --> 00:15:19,537
How does a step fail?

266
00:15:19,857 --> 00:15:22,203
Every command ends with an exit code.

267
00:15:22,523 --> 00:15:24,017
Zero opens the gate.

268
00:15:24,337 --> 00:15:25,628
Anything else shuts it,

269
00:15:25,628 --> 00:15:29,051
and the red climbs from the step
to the job to the whole run.

270
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:32,991
Run 1, the Black step:
would reformat utils.py.

271
00:15:33,311 --> 00:15:35,188
1 file would be reformatted.

272
00:15:35,508 --> 00:15:37,748
Exit code 1, so the gate shuts.

273
00:15:37,983 --> 00:15:40,138
The step list shows what happened next.

274
00:15:40,458 --> 00:15:41,588
Black, failure.

275
00:15:41,908 --> 00:15:43,210
Flake8, skipped.

276
00:15:43,530 --> 00:15:48,031
The steps below a failed step don't run,
unless they ask to run always.

277
00:15:48,267 --> 00:15:53,732
The cause is the mess you met in lecture 9,
still in the repository on purpose:

278
00:15:53,732 --> 00:15:58,229
no space after the comma,
and three blank lines where two belong.

279
00:15:58,467 --> 00:16:00,451
What would the skipped Flake8 have said?

280
00:16:00,771 --> 00:16:04,056
An extra run shows two warnings
on those same lines.

281
00:16:04,376 --> 00:16:08,216
But even then it's advisory:
only Black decides the job.

282
00:16:08,450 --> 00:16:10,824
So that's the whole rule: a step fails

283
00:16:10,824 --> 00:16:13,698
when its command exits
with anything but zero.

284
00:16:14,018 --> 00:16:16,173
It's the only thing CI listens to.

285
00:16:17,717 --> 00:16:18,869
Now the tests.

286
00:16:19,189 --> 00:16:22,320
The lecture's one test box
is three jobs here,

287
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:25,248
side by side, each at a different altitude.

288
00:16:25,484 --> 00:16:30,135
Unit tests check one function at a time,
with no outside dependencies.

289
00:16:30,455 --> 00:16:33,569
The log collects 11 tests, and 11 pass.

290
00:16:33,817 --> 00:16:37,700
Then the coverage step runs them again,
and all 11 pass again.

291
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:40,246
But pytest hits an internal error:

292
00:16:40,246 --> 00:16:43,652
permission denied,
writing into the coverage folder.

293
00:16:43,972 --> 00:16:45,166
Exit code 3.

294
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:46,872
Yet the job is green.

295
00:16:47,192 --> 00:16:51,053
The step says continue-on-error,
so the failure is shunted aside.

296
00:16:51,373 --> 00:16:54,778
Then the upload, marked if:
always(), finds no files;

297
00:16:54,778 --> 00:16:57,091
on GitHub, a yellow warning says so.

298
00:16:57,334 --> 00:17:03,073
The README has a small gap:
it promises terminal, XML and HTML reports.

299
00:17:03,393 --> 00:17:06,529
Docker creates
the missing coverage folder as root,

300
00:17:06,529 --> 00:17:09,665
and the container's user,
app, can't write into it.

301
00:17:09,985 --> 00:17:14,102
Create the folder writable first;
the exercises have the fix.

302
00:17:14,350 --> 00:17:17,851
Integration tests use
the FastAPI test client:

303
00:17:17,851 --> 00:17:21,582
the app is called inside
the same Python process.

304
00:17:21,902 --> 00:17:24,825
No server, no port. 10 passed.

305
00:17:25,067 --> 00:17:27,243
System tests need the real thing.

306
00:17:27,563 --> 00:17:29,562
One container starts the server

307
00:17:29,562 --> 00:17:33,174
in the background and publishes
port 9000 on the runner.

308
00:17:33,494 --> 00:17:34,812
An extra command,

309
00:17:34,812 --> 00:17:38,379
docker ps, shows it running,
with 9000 mapped.

310
00:17:38,617 --> 00:17:44,441
Then the job waits 10 seconds,
and curl -f knocks on localhost:9000.

311
00:17:44,761 --> 00:17:48,708
Only when the welcome message
comes back does the next gate open.

312
00:17:48,950 --> 00:17:52,696
A second container runs the tests
with --network host:

313
00:17:52,696 --> 00:17:57,483
it shares the runner's network,
so localhost:9000 reaches the server.

314
00:17:57,803 --> 00:17:58,892
5 passed.

315
00:17:59,134 --> 00:18:04,632
Two steps marked if: always() come last,
even after a failure: show the server log,

316
00:18:04,632 --> 00:18:09,203
where every test request arrives,
then stop and remove the container.

317
00:18:09,523 --> 00:18:12,104
That's the README's conditional execution.

318
00:18:12,350 --> 00:18:16,275
In lecture 9, a missing server
made these tests skip quietly.

319
00:18:16,595 --> 00:18:19,476
Here the curl gate
makes it fail loudly instead.

320
00:18:21,117 --> 00:18:22,597
Last job: the summary,

321
00:18:22,597 --> 00:18:27,240
which the lecture says collects
the results and reports pass or fail.

322
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:30,034
Its needs line names the three test jobs.

323
00:18:30,354 --> 00:18:33,929
And if: always() lets it run
even when one of them failed:

324
00:18:33,929 --> 00:18:36,456
it runs, which isn't the same as passing.

325
00:18:36,700 --> 00:18:39,131
Its script prints each job's result,

326
00:18:39,131 --> 00:18:42,507
writes a status
to $GITHUB_OUTPUT for later steps,

327
00:18:42,507 --> 00:18:45,511
and exits with 1
if any result isn't success.

328
00:18:45,750 --> 00:18:50,593
In run 1, three success tokens arrive:
unit, integration, system.

329
00:18:50,913 --> 00:18:52,214
All tests passed.

330
00:18:52,450 --> 00:18:54,285
And here is the hook, answered.

331
00:18:54,605 --> 00:18:58,359
Lint isn't in needs,
so its failure never reaches the summary.

332
00:18:58,679 --> 00:18:59,957
The run is still red,

333
00:18:59,957 --> 00:19:02,818
because any failed job
turns the whole run red.

334
00:19:03,067 --> 00:19:07,151
The README says the summary checks
the results of all test jobs,

335
00:19:07,151 --> 00:19:10,278
and that's literally true:
lint isn't a test job.

336
00:19:10,598 --> 00:19:14,608
Add lint and format to needs
and to the check if you want it counted.

337
00:19:14,850 --> 00:19:16,343
Now the bug from class.

338
00:19:16,663 --> 00:19:17,861
In the demo commit,

339
00:19:17,861 --> 00:19:22,338
the distance function takes
the square root of 2 times z, instead of z.

340
00:19:22,658 --> 00:19:24,877
We ran this one for you: just watch.

341
00:19:25,117 --> 00:19:26,546
Predict before the run.

342
00:19:26,866 --> 00:19:30,216
Which jobs turn red now,
and what does Test Summary say?

343
00:19:39,050 --> 00:19:42,143
Integration: red, 1 failed and 9 passed.

344
00:19:42,463 --> 00:19:46,432
The distance for 3 and 4 came back
as about 7, not 5.

345
00:19:46,667 --> 00:19:50,133
System: red, 2 failed and 3 passed:
3 and 4 again,

346
00:19:50,133 --> 00:19:54,710
and the default point, 1 and 2,
which came back too large as well.

347
00:19:55,030 --> 00:19:59,104
Unit: green, because only
the power function has unit tests.

348
00:19:59,424 --> 00:20:02,198
Lint: red anyway, for utils.py.

349
00:20:02,434 --> 00:20:06,061
And the summary now says some tests failed,
and exits with 1.

350
00:20:06,381 --> 00:20:08,087
This time the summary heard it.

351
00:20:09,633 --> 00:20:11,894
Back to run 1 and the lecture's loop.

352
00:20:12,214 --> 00:20:14,187
The notification came back red,

353
00:20:14,187 --> 00:20:17,625
so now the developer fixes
the code and commits again,

354
00:20:17,625 --> 00:20:19,852
and the loop goes around once more.

355
00:20:20,100 --> 00:20:22,493
The fix is the tool from lecture 9,

356
00:20:22,493 --> 00:20:26,457
Part 4 of the same README,
so these steps are extras here.

357
00:20:26,777 --> 00:20:29,959
pip install pre-commit,
then pre-commit install,

358
00:20:29,959 --> 00:20:32,345
which puts the hook into .git/hooks.

359
00:20:32,583 --> 00:20:34,738
Then pre-commit run --all-files.

360
00:20:35,058 --> 00:20:36,856
The hygiene checks pass;

361
00:20:36,856 --> 00:20:41,500
Black fails, because it modified a file:
reformatted utils.py.

362
00:20:41,820 --> 00:20:43,399
Flake8 then passes.

363
00:20:43,719 --> 00:20:44,871
Pause and run it.

364
00:20:45,116 --> 00:20:47,972
git diff shows exactly what Black changed:

365
00:20:47,972 --> 00:20:51,303
a space after the comma,
and one blank line gone.

366
00:20:51,550 --> 00:20:53,790
Run it again, and every hook passes.

367
00:20:54,110 --> 00:20:55,731
Nothing is left to fix.

368
00:20:56,150 --> 00:20:59,577
Now git add .,
and git commit with a message:

369
00:20:59,577 --> 00:21:01,633
Format utils.py with Black.

370
00:21:01,953 --> 00:21:04,598
The message is ours; the README has none.

371
00:21:04,918 --> 00:21:06,838
The hooks run on the commit and pass.

372
00:21:07,158 --> 00:21:08,310
Then git push.

373
00:21:08,630 --> 00:21:10,059
Pause and push yours.

374
00:21:10,300 --> 00:21:12,113
Run 2: all six green.

375
00:21:12,433 --> 00:21:17,041
In the lint job, Black is happy,
and this time Flake8 runs too, and passes.

376
00:21:17,361 --> 00:21:20,284
On a fork, this is your first run,
and it's green.

377
00:21:20,533 --> 00:21:23,242
Now your run matches
the README's screenshot.

378
00:21:23,562 --> 00:21:24,973
One turn of the loop:

379
00:21:24,973 --> 00:21:28,533
a red notification,
a fix, a commit, and a green run.

380
00:21:29,983 --> 00:21:32,735
The lecture calls this one
the local conundrum.

381
00:21:33,055 --> 00:21:37,812
If the hooks already ran on your laptop,
why run the same checks again on a server?

382
00:21:38,050 --> 00:21:40,845
These steps are extras,
and you can just watch.

383
00:21:41,165 --> 00:21:45,403
A quick edit squeezes
the spaces out of one line in service.py,

384
00:21:45,403 --> 00:21:46,882
and git diff shows it.

385
00:21:47,116 --> 00:21:50,316
git add ., then git commit -m "quick fix".

386
00:21:50,636 --> 00:21:54,946
The Black hook fails and reformats the file,
so the hook stopped the commit.

387
00:21:55,883 --> 00:21:59,766
git status --short shows the letter M,
for modified, twice.

388
00:22:00,086 --> 00:22:04,438
Black fixed your working copy,
but the staged copy is still the messy one.

389
00:22:04,683 --> 00:22:09,419
Now the lecture's command:
git commit --no-verify -m "quick fix".

390
00:22:09,739 --> 00:22:14,774
No hook ran, and the commit exists:
--no-verify skips every pre-commit check.

391
00:22:15,050 --> 00:22:16,050
We push it.

392
00:22:16,309 --> 00:22:19,786
Run 3: lint and format, red, on service.py.

393
00:22:20,106 --> 00:22:22,218
The tests and the summary all pass.

394
00:22:22,883 --> 00:22:26,104
The skipped Flake8 would have flagged
that line three times.

395
00:22:26,424 --> 00:22:31,310
CI caught what --no-verify let through:
slide 31, at your terminal.

396
00:22:31,550 --> 00:22:33,043
Clean up the experiment.

397
00:22:33,363 --> 00:22:37,651
Here, git restore only throws away
Black's fix in the working copy.

398
00:22:37,971 --> 00:22:39,310
If you followed along,

399
00:22:39,310 --> 00:22:42,473
the messy commit is on your main now,
pushed or not.

400
00:22:42,793 --> 00:22:43,987
Not pushed yet?

401
00:22:44,307 --> 00:22:47,209
git reset --hard HEAD~1 drops it.

402
00:22:47,529 --> 00:22:48,529
Pushed?

403
00:22:48,595 --> 00:22:53,864
Run pre-commit run --all-files,
then add, commit and push, before part 6.

404
00:22:54,100 --> 00:22:59,092
But CI repeats only Black and Flake8,
and only on the api/ folder.

405
00:22:59,412 --> 00:23:05,076
The seven hygiene hooks never run in CI:
trailing whitespace, end of file,

406
00:23:05,076 --> 00:23:09,438
YAML, large files,
JSON, merge markers, and private keys.

407
00:23:09,683 --> 00:23:13,608
One more gap: the hooks pin an older
Black than the image installs.

408
00:23:13,928 --> 00:23:17,683
At the time of recording they agreed,
but they can drift apart.

409
00:23:17,933 --> 00:23:22,520
A teammate commits a private key file
with --no-verify and pushes to main.

410
00:23:22,840 --> 00:23:25,058
Which job of this pipeline catches it?

411
00:23:33,900 --> 00:23:37,740
None. The detect-private-key hook
is a local hook only.

412
00:23:38,060 --> 00:23:41,690
The fix is one CI step,
pre-commit run --all-files:

413
00:23:41,690 --> 00:23:46,316
the very step that fails
on the lecture's imagined Slack message.

414
00:23:46,636 --> 00:23:49,388
And rotate that key; it's already on GitHub.

415
00:23:50,733 --> 00:23:54,466
That imagined message brings us back
to the lecture's webhooks.

416
00:23:54,786 --> 00:23:58,134
A webhook is GitHub
calling an outside address

417
00:23:58,134 --> 00:23:59,808
when something happens:

418
00:23:59,808 --> 00:24:03,149
to start an outside CI
system such as Jenkins,

419
00:24:03,149 --> 00:24:05,474
to deploy, or to post to a chat.

420
00:24:05,716 --> 00:24:09,257
This part is optional:
it needs your own Slack workspace.

421
00:24:09,577 --> 00:24:12,009
And this webhook works the other way round.

422
00:24:12,329 --> 00:24:15,267
Slack gives you an incoming webhook,
an address,

423
00:24:15,267 --> 00:24:18,388
and the last steps
of the test summary job call it.

424
00:24:18,708 --> 00:24:21,588
GitHub doesn't ring this bell;
your workflow does.

425
00:24:21,833 --> 00:24:25,746
The lecture imagines a Slack
channel told about every push,

426
00:24:25,746 --> 00:24:29,790
failures included, with the failed step,
counts and duration.

427
00:24:30,110 --> 00:24:34,142
This workflow sends less:
passed or failed, the repository,

428
00:24:34,142 --> 00:24:38,516
branch, commit and author,
one mark per test job, and two links.

429
00:24:38,750 --> 00:24:40,734
The webhook address is a secret.

430
00:24:41,054 --> 00:24:45,118
Add it under Settings →
Secrets and variables → Actions,

431
00:24:45,118 --> 00:24:50,270
as a new repository secret named
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL, not under Webhooks.

432
00:24:50,590 --> 00:24:54,387
It never goes in the repository,
logs show it as ***,

433
00:24:54,387 --> 00:24:57,395
and pull requests from forks don't get it.

434
00:24:57,633 --> 00:25:00,345
The check step tests
whether the secret is set,

435
00:25:00,345 --> 00:25:02,710
and writes enabled=true or enabled=false.

436
00:25:03,030 --> 00:25:05,505
Why not test the secret
right in the if line?

437
00:25:05,825 --> 00:25:08,012
GitHub doesn't allow secrets there;

438
00:25:08,012 --> 00:25:12,822
the repository's own history tried it,
and replaced it with this shell check.

439
00:25:13,066 --> 00:25:16,149
In run 1 there's no secret,
so enabled is false,

440
00:25:16,149 --> 00:25:19,168
and the Slack step is skipped,
as it should be.

441
00:25:19,416 --> 00:25:20,696
Now the demo run.

442
00:25:21,016 --> 00:25:22,723
Check test results fails.

443
00:25:23,043 --> 00:25:26,484
The check step has no if:
always(), so it's skipped too,

444
00:25:26,484 --> 00:25:30,232
enabled stays empty,
and the Slack step's condition is false.

445
00:25:30,466 --> 00:25:33,197
As written,
the bell only rings for good news.

446
00:25:33,517 --> 00:25:36,695
The fix is one line: if:
always(), on the check step.

447
00:25:37,015 --> 00:25:39,554
The exercises have a hardened version.

448
00:25:40,900 --> 00:25:44,676
Part 6 of the README is optional,
and it's the lecture's gate.

449
00:25:44,996 --> 00:25:47,727
A push-triggered run
is the post-receive bell.

450
00:25:48,047 --> 00:25:52,248
On GitHub.com,
the gate that refuses is branch protection,

451
00:25:52,248 --> 00:25:54,276
with required status checks.

452
00:25:54,517 --> 00:25:57,632
Go to Settings → Branches,
and add a rule for main.

453
00:25:57,952 --> 00:26:00,874
Tick the boxes "Require
a pull request before merging",

454
00:26:00,874 --> 00:26:03,584
and "Require status checks
to pass before merging".

455
00:26:03,904 --> 00:26:08,234
Today's page may call it a classic rule,
or offer rulesets instead.

456
00:26:08,483 --> 00:26:09,784
Then pick the checks.

457
00:26:10,104 --> 00:26:13,690
A small slip:
the picker lists jobs by their names,

458
00:26:13,690 --> 00:26:18,190
like Lint and Format Check and Unit Tests,
not the README's IDs.

459
00:26:18,510 --> 00:26:20,707
They appear only after a recent run.

460
00:26:21,027 --> 00:26:24,227
On a private repository
this needs a paid plan.

461
00:26:24,467 --> 00:26:28,542
Then test it:
git checkout -b feature/test-protection.

462
00:26:28,862 --> 00:26:32,647
Pushing that branch starts nothing,
as you saw with the triggers;

463
00:26:32,647 --> 00:26:35,325
the run starts
when you open the pull request.

464
00:26:35,567 --> 00:26:39,556
The README breaks the tests
by appending def broken(): assert False.

465
00:26:39,876 --> 00:26:43,077
An extra run in the image
still says 11 passed,

466
00:26:43,077 --> 00:26:47,641
because pytest only collects functions
whose names start with test.

467
00:26:47,883 --> 00:26:51,019
So name it test_broken instead,
an extra fix.

468
00:26:51,339 --> 00:26:53,366
Now 1 failed and 11 passed.

469
00:26:53,686 --> 00:26:56,416
That's the one
that turns the pull request red,

470
00:26:56,416 --> 00:26:59,553
and with protection on,
the merge button stays locked.

471
00:26:59,800 --> 00:27:03,924
Then git add .,
git commit -m "Intentionally break tests",

472
00:27:03,924 --> 00:27:07,053
and git push origin feature/test-protection.

473
00:27:07,373 --> 00:27:12,323
Open the pull request on GitHub:
the merge stays locked until the tests pass.

474
00:27:12,643 --> 00:27:14,328
Pause and try it on yours.

475
00:27:14,648 --> 00:27:17,293
Here, git checkout --
puts the test file back.

476
00:27:18,433 --> 00:27:20,452
The twist the lecture promised:

477
00:27:20,452 --> 00:27:24,620
non-determinism enters every stage
when the code trains a model.

478
00:27:24,940 --> 00:27:29,433
Its answers: version everything;
automate retraining and testing,

479
00:27:29,433 --> 00:27:33,580
which Milestone 5 asks for;
monitor; and roll out with care.

480
00:27:33,816 --> 00:27:37,251
Continuous deployment,
the lecture's last section,

481
00:27:37,251 --> 00:27:40,067
automates the release: that's lecture 23.

482
00:27:40,316 --> 00:27:41,852
Clean up: switch to main,

483
00:27:41,852 --> 00:27:44,924
and delete
the feature/test-protection branch here

484
00:27:44,924 --> 00:27:47,591
and on GitHub; that closes its pull request.

485
00:27:47,911 --> 00:27:49,217
Remove the branch rule,

486
00:27:49,217 --> 00:27:52,113
and the Slack secret and webhook,
if you made them.

487
00:27:52,433 --> 00:27:55,281
Disable the workflow
on a private repository,

488
00:27:55,281 --> 00:27:57,938
and run the Docker cleanup from lecture 9.

489
00:27:58,183 --> 00:27:59,442
Once more, the loop.

490
00:27:59,762 --> 00:28:01,234
Your push is the event.

491
00:28:01,554 --> 00:28:05,906
Each job gets a fresh machine,
so the image travels as an artifact.

492
00:28:06,226 --> 00:28:09,170
Every step is a gate
that reads an exit code,

493
00:28:09,170 --> 00:28:12,114
and the summary hears
only the jobs it needs.

494
00:28:12,350 --> 00:28:14,291
Test yourself, pausing on each.

495
00:28:14,611 --> 00:28:18,096
One: name two ways code
reaches the shared repository

496
00:28:18,096 --> 00:28:20,265
without passing your local hooks.

497
00:28:20,585 --> 00:28:22,739
Which checks does this pipeline repeat?

498
00:28:22,983 --> 00:28:26,588
Two: which unit gets a fresh machine,
a job or a step?

499
00:28:26,908 --> 00:28:30,172
And why does that force a docker load
in every check job?

500
00:28:30,416 --> 00:28:34,384
Three: your run is red,
but the summary says all tests passed.

501
00:28:34,704 --> 00:28:36,966
Why, and what is the one-line fix?

502
00:28:37,200 --> 00:28:40,325
Four:
why can Flake8 fail without failing its job,

503
00:28:40,325 --> 00:28:43,450
and why don't you see its output
when Black fails?

504
00:28:44,183 --> 00:28:48,255
Five: is a push-triggered run a pre-receive
or a post-receive check,

505
00:28:48,255 --> 00:28:49,751
and what makes it a gate?

506
00:28:50,071 --> 00:28:53,250
Answers are in the exercises
and the study guide.

